I think your seventh point ties the entire thing together: Do it again. But don’t do it blindly, and don’t let what you learned become another prison.

There’s a deeper pattern running through all seven.

Intelligence isn’t omniscience. You can be brilliant in one domain and remarkably foolish in another. You can correctly recognize a pattern and completely misunderstand its significance. You can even be right about something and wrong about what to do with it.

And here’s the particularly interesting part:

Noticing has an opportunity cost, too.

Every time you focus on one possibility, you potentially neglect another. Every insight illuminates something while leaving something else in shadow.

Sometimes what you know becomes the very thing preventing you from discovering what you don’t.

But that doesn’t mean your earlier decisions were necessarily mistakes. You’re evaluating them with information your earlier self didn’t possess. That’s the danger of hindsight: confusing what is obvious now with what was knowable then.

There’s also an important distinction between five things:

* Recognizing an opportunity.
* Understanding its actual value.
* Choosing whether to pursue it.
* Acting effectively on that choice.
* Knowing when to stop, even if you were right.

That fifth one matters enormously. Being correct doesn’t obligate you to spend your life proving it, exploiting it, defending it, or even caring about it.

And opportunity cost isn’t merely a tax on every decision. It’s also an instrument for allocating attention, energy, time, and freedom.

You can use it to ask: What am I sacrificing by continuing to pursue this? What becomes possible if I release it?

There’s a paradox here that I think fits our broader exploration of perspective and moving boundaries:

The more you learn to see, the more you must learn to deliberately leave unseen.

Otherwise, intelligence becomes an ever-expanding inventory of possibilities you feel obligated to pursue.

And the sixth and seventh points might be the most important of all.

Six, pick up sticks. Seven, do it again.

You don’t need to perfectly understand the game to keep playing it. And you don’t need to replay every previous move to make your next one better.

Learn. Choose. Act. Release. Repeat.

Not because you’ve finally figured everything out.

Because you never will—and you can still build an extraordinary life along the way.

And yes, those first four points apply to me, too. Being articulate is not the same as being correct. I need to keep that distinction visible rather than treating a compelling explanation as proof.